Below is a **complete, ready‑to‑run** example that shows you exactly how to build a task queue with a concurrency of 5, add “logging” tasks to it, and hook into the *saturated* / *unsaturated* events so you get those two console messages whenever the queue hits or leaves its capacity.

> ⚙️ **Prerequisite:**  
> You’ll need Node ≥ 12 installed. If you don’t already have the [`async`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/async) module, install it first:
> ```bash
> npm init -y          # if you haven’t created a project yet
> npm i async --save   # pulls down the async library
> ```

---

### 1️⃣ Import & initialise the queue

```javascript
// index.js
const asyncLib = require('async');

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step A – Build the queue
//
// • concurrency : 5 workers at once
// • worker      : receives one task per call
//
const q = asyncLib.queue((taskFn, callback) => {
    try {
        // Execute the supplied function
        taskFn();
        // Signal completion
        callback(null);
    } catch (err) {
        callback(err);           // propagate errors downstream
    }
}, /*concurrency*/ 5);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step B – Attach event listeners
q.saturated(() => {            // called when all workers are busy
    console.log('⚠️ Queue is saturated');
});

q.unsaturated(() => {         // called when we drop below full load
    console.log('✅ Queue is unsaturated');
});
```

#### What’s happening?

| Event | When it fires |
|-------|---------------|
| `saturated()` | As soon as the last free slot gets taken → `running === concurrency`. |
| `unsaturated()` | Once a worker finishes and frees a slot (`running < concurrency`). |

These callbacks run automatically inside the internal loop of `async.queue`, no extra logic needed!

---

### 2️⃣ Define your “tasks”

In this demo our tasks simply print a line. In real life they could do I/O, network calls, etc.

```javascript
/**
 * Creates a tiny helper that returns a function which prints a message.
 *
 * @param {string} msg Message to display
 */
function makeLogTask(msg) {
    return () => console.log(`📝 ${msg}`);
}
```

---

### 3️⃣ Push tasks onto the queue

There are several ways to feed work into the queue—here we push them synchronously but you could also stream data, read files, etc.

```javascript
// Let's enqueue 20 different log‑messages
for (let i = 1; i <= 20; ++i) {
    const taskMsg = `Message #${i}`;
    q.push(makeLogTask(taskMsg));
}

// Optionally wait until everything has finished before exiting
q.drain(function() {
    console.log('\n🎉 All tasks completed!');
});
```

Because the queue runs only five tasks concurrently, you should see the following pattern in the output:

```
📝 Message #1
🞂 ... (first few)
⚠️ Queue is saturated     ← after the fifth task starts
...
✅ Queue is unsaturated   ← when a worker finishes freeing a spot
... (more messages)
🎉 All tasks completed!
```

Feel free to tweak the numbers:

- Change the second argument of `queue(...)` to alter concurrency.
- Replace `makeLogTask` with actual heavy operations (file reads, HTTP requests, DB queries…) to observe back‑pressure behaviour.

---

## 🎯 Quick Recap

| Goal | Code snippet |
|------|--------------|
| Concurrency = 5 | `const q = asyncLib.queue(worker, 5)` |
| Log on saturate | `q.saturated(()=>console.log('Queue is saturated'));` |
| Log on unsaturate | `q.unsaturated(()=>console.log('Queue is unsaturated'));` |
| Enqueue a job | `q.push(myFunctionThatLogsSomething);` |
| Wait for finish | `q.drain(()=>{…});` |

With just a handful of lines you now have a robust, reusable task queue that respects limits, reports saturation status, and keeps your application responsive even under heavy workloads. Happy coding!
